msgctxt "#30027"
msgid "Live TV pause/rewind (timeshift)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30028"
msgid "Off (default): a plain live stream straight from Dispatcharr's own proxy, no pause/rewind, no extra addon, no admin account needed -- works out of the box. Local: real pause/rewind, buffered on this device via the separate inputstream.ffmpegdirect addon (Kodi's official repository, must be installed or live channel playback fails outright) -- no Dispatcharr admin account needed either, but the buffer doesn't persist across a Kodi restart or follow you to another device. Server-side: real pause/rewind/fast-forward too, buffered by Dispatcharr itself via this addon's companion \"Timeshift Buffer\" plugin (installed separately on your Dispatcharr instance, not through Kodi) -- press Play as normal, no extra step, and the buffer is shared across every device using this Dispatcharr account. Requires the plugin's own HTTP port to be reachable from this device (see the plugin's own README for the container port-mapping it needs), and requires the account this addon is configured with to be a Dispatcharr admin -- the plugin run/ API rejects anything less, and every live channel will fail to play until that's set up. Pick Local instead of Server-side if you want real pause/rewind without granting admin access or installing a Dispatcharr-side plugin."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30035"
msgid "Recordings"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30038"
msgid "Recordings/timers refresh interval (minutes)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30039"
msgid "How often to ask Kodi to re-check its recordings and timers list on its own, without waiting for a local action (like starting or deleting a recording from this Kodi) to prompt it. Without this, a change made another way -- from a different Kodi install sharing this Dispatcharr account, directly through Dispatcharr itself, or a recording finishing on its own with nothing local to react to it -- would only ever show up after restarting Kodi."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30040"
msgid "Enable real-time recording/timer updates (experimental)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30041"
msgid "Connects directly to Dispatcharr's own real-time update feed (the same one its own web interface uses -- no extra setup on the Dispatcharr side needed) so a change shows up in Kodi immediately instead of waiting for the refresh interval above. That interval keeps running as a fallback even with this on, in case the connection can't be made or drops and stays down. Experimental: this is new, hand-written networking code that hasn't seen the same real-world use as the rest of this addon -- leave off if you'd rather stick with the plain refresh interval. Needs a Kodi restart to take effect either way (starting/stopping this connection isn't something this addon can do while already running) -- Kodi will prompt you when you save."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30049"
msgid "Recurring timer: manual timezone offset from UTC (minutes)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30050"
msgid "Only used when the timezone setting above is left on \"Manual\" -- either because Dispatcharr's configured zone isn't one of the common ones this addon knows real DST rules for, or you've deliberately chosen manual control. Only affects recurring (day-of-week) timers. Dispatcharr stores a recurring rule's start/end time-of-day as a plain wall-clock value with no timezone attached, and interprets it using its own configured system timezone (Settings -> System in Dispatcharr's web UI), not UTC -- this addon has no way to derive an arbitrary zone's current offset itself (that needs a full IANA timezone database it doesn't bundle), so when the dropdown above can't handle it, it falls back to you entering it directly: set this to match Dispatcharr's configured system timezone's CURRENT offset from UTC, e.g. -360 for US Central Standard Time (UTC-6) or -300 for Central Daylight Time (UTC-5) -- if that zone observes daylight saving, you'll need to update this by 60 minutes twice a year yourself, or a recurring timer will fire an hour off until you do. Leave at 0 if Dispatcharr's system timezone is UTC. One-time and series timers are unaffected -- this only matters for the recurring timer type."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30051"
msgid "Off"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#30052"
